ProsperoSunshine: pairing at home only unless the owner allows the internet, and pending pairings by address

Pairing goes over plain HTTP with a four-digit PIN, which a man in the middle can try offline. With the ports forwarded, any
stranger could also start pairings (a card and a chime in the app each time), hold the uniqueid every Moonlight sends
(0123456789ABCDEF) so that the owner's Moonlight got 409 for five minutes, and with a later step end or use up the owner's
pending pairing. Now:
- /pair from an address outside the LAN and Tailscale (net::from_address) is refused with 403 and a message Moonlight shows,
  unless pair_over_internet = enabled in sunshine.conf (off by default, an option in the app and on the web page).
- A pending pairing is keyed by the address it came from and its uniqueid (src/net/exposure.hpp), so requests from another
  address neither find, end, use up nor block it. Moonlight sends every step of a pairing from the same address.
- One address holds at most two pending pairings; Sunshine's limit of 32 for all stays.
Somebody behind the owner's own address (the same NAT) is still indistinguishable; the PIN is what pairs.

